Q: How can inline functions exist in a lexically scoped language? A: <p>Inline functions are not macros, they aren't substituted as source text. Rather, the <em>compiled</em> code is inserted at code generation time. So there isn't any problem with lexical scoping, local variable names have been converted to stack frame offsets by the time the substitution occurs.</p>
<p>But if you want to imagine that it does text substitution, you can also imagine that local variables first get replaced with unique names. So your main function becomes something like:</p>
<pre><code>fun main() {
    val __local_main_text = &quot;hi&quot;
    someFunction()
}
</code></pre>
<p>This is similar to the way <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygienic_macro" rel="nofollow noreferrer">&quot;hygienic&quot; macros</a> avoid unwanted identifier capture.</p>

Q: How to configure ESLint for React in the Atom editor A: <p>To get ESLint working nicely with React:</p>
<ol>
<li>Install the linter and linter-eslint plugins</li>
<li>Run <code>npm install eslint-plugin-react</code></li>
<li>Add <code>&quot;plugins&quot;: [&quot;react&quot;]</code> to your <em>.eslintrc</em> configuration file</li>
<li>Add <code>&quot;ecmaFeatures&quot;: {&quot;jsx&quot;: true}</code> to your <em>.eslintrc</em> configuration file</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is an example of a <em>.eslintrc</em> configuration file:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;env&quot;: {
        &quot;browser&quot;: true,
        &quot;node&quot;: true
    },

    &quot;globals&quot;: {
        &quot;React&quot;: true
    },

    &quot;ecmaFeatures&quot;: {
        &quot;jsx&quot;: true
    },

    &quot;plugins&quot;: [
        &quot;react&quot;
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>I am using ESLint v1.1.0 at the moment.</p>
<p><em>Side note</em>:</p>
<p>I had to install both ESLint and eslint-plugin-react as project dependencies (e.g., <code>npm install eslint eslint-plugin-react --save-dev</code>).</p>
